Rule.fixtext:
Run the following command to remove the selinux=0 and enforcing=0 parameters: # grubby --update-kernel ALL --remove-args "selinux=0 enforcing=0" Run the following command to remove the selinux=0 and enforcing=0 parameters if they were created by the deprecated grub2-mkconfig command: # grep -Prsq -- '^\h*([^#
]+\h+)?kernelopts=([^#
]+\h+)?(selinux|enforcing)=0 ' /boot/grub2 /boot/efi && grub2-mkconfig -o "$(grep -Prl -- '\h*([^#
]+\h+)?kernelopts=([^#
]+\h+)?(selinux|enforcing)=0 ' /boot/grub2 /boot/efi)"

check.check-content:
Run the following command to verify that rescue mode requires authentication:
# systemctl show -p ExecStart --value rescue.service
The returned ExecStart should invoke sulogin (for example via systemd-sulogin- shell). Example output:
{ path=/usr/lib/systemd/systemd-sulogin-shell ;
argv[]=/usr/lib/systemd/systemd-sulogin-shell rescue ; ignore_errors=yes ;
... }
If the returned ExecStart does not invoke sulogin, authentication is not required for rescue mode.

Run the following command to verify that emergency mode requires authentication:
# systemctl show -p ExecStart --value emergency.service
The returned ExecStart should invoke sulogin (for example via systemd-sulogin- shell). Example output:
{ path=/usr/lib/systemd/systemd-sulogin-shell ;
argv[]=/usr/lib/systemd/systemd-sulogin-shell emergency ; ignore_errors=yes ;
... }
If the returned ExecStart does not invoke sulogin, authentication is not required for emergency mode.

Rule.fixtext:
Run the following script to set the owner and restrict permissions on each SSH private host key file from the running sshd configuration:
#!/usr/bin/env bash
{
while IFS= read -r l_file; do
ssh-keygen -lf "$l_file" &>/dev/null || continue
chown root:root "$l_file"
chmod u-x,go-rwx "$l_file"
done < <(sshd -T 2>/dev/null | awk '$1=="hostkey"{print $2}')
}
• OR - Where host-based authentication requires the ssh_keys group to read the host keys, set group ownership to ssh_keys with mode 0640 or more restrictive instead:
#!/usr/bin/env bash
{
while IFS= read -r l_file; do
ssh-keygen -lf "$l_file" &>/dev/null || continue
chown root:ssh_keys "$l_file"
chmod u-x,g-wx,o-rwx "$l_file"
done < <(sshd -T 2>/dev/null | awk '$1=="hostkey"{print $2}')
}

Run the following script to set the owner, group, and restrict permissions on each SSH public host key file from the running sshd configuration:
#!/usr/bin/env bash
{
while IFS= read -r l_key; do
l_file="${l_key}.pub"
[ -e "$l_file" ] || continue
chown root:root "$l_file"
chmod u-x,go-wx "$l_file"
done < <(sshd -T 2>/dev/null | awk '$1=="hostkey"{print $2}')
}

Run the following command to set the shell for any service accounts returned by the audit to nologin:
# usermod -s $(command -v nologin) <user>
Example script:
#!/usr/bin/env bash
{
l_valid_shells="^($(awk -F\/ '($NF != "nologin" && $NF != "false")
{print}' /etc/shells | sed -rn '/^\//{s,/,\\\\/,g;p}' | paste -s -d '|' -
))$"
awk -v pat="$l_valid_shells" -F:
'($1!~/^(root|halt|sync|shutdown|nfsnobody)$/ && ($3<'"$(awk
'/^\s*UID_MIN/{print $2}' /etc/login.defs)"' || $3 == 65534) && $(NF) ~ pat)
{system ("usermod -s '"$(command -v nologin)"' " $1)}' /etc/passwd
}

check.check-content:
Run the following command to verify system accounts, except for root, halt, sync, shutdown or nfsnobody, do not have a valid login shell:
#!/usr/bin/env bash
{
l_valid_shells="^($(awk -F\/ '($NF != "nologin" && $NF != "false")
{print}' /etc/shells | sed -rn '/^\//{s,/,\\\\/,g;p}' | paste -s -d '|' -
))$"
awk -v pat="$l_valid_shells" -F:
'($1!~/^(root|halt|sync|shutdown|nfsnobody)$/ && ($3<'"$(awk
'/^\s*UID_MIN/{print $2}' /etc/login.defs)"' || $3 == 65534) && $(NF) ~ pat)
{print "Service account: \"" $1 "\" has a valid shell: " $7}' /etc/passwd
}
Nothing should be returned

Rule.fixtext:
Run the following command to lock any non-root accounts without a valid login shell returned by the audit:
# usermod -L <user>
Example script:
#!/usr/bin/env bash
{
l_valid_shells="^($(awk -F\/ '($NF != "nologin" && $NF != "false")
{print}' /etc/shells | sed -rn '/^\//{s,/,\\\\/,g;p}' | paste -s -d '|' -
))$"
while IFS= read -r l_user; do
passwd -S "$l_user" | awk '$2 !~ /^L/ {system ("usermod -L " $1)}'
done < <(awk -v pat="$l_valid_shells" -F: '($1 != "root" && $(NF) !~ pat)
{print $1}' /etc/passwd)
}

check.check-content:
Run the following script to verify all non-root accounts without a valid login shell are locked.
#!/usr/bin/env bash
{
l_valid_shells="^($(awk -F\/ '($NF != "nologin" && $NF != "false")
{print}' /etc/shells | sed -rn '/^\//{s,/,\\\\/,g;p}' | paste -s -d '|' -
))$"
while IFS= read -r l_user; do
passwd -S "$l_user" | awk '$2 !~ /^L/ {print "Account: \"" $1 "\" does
not have a valid login shell and is not locked"}'
done < <(awk -v pat="$l_valid_shells" -F: '($1 != "root" && $(NF) !~ pat)
{print $1}' /etc/passwd)
}
Nothing should be returned

Run the following script to verify that TMOUT is set to a value greater than 0 and no larger than 900 seconds, and is marked readonly and exported, in each shell initialization file that configures it: #!/usr/bin/env bash { l_max=900 a_files=() while IFS= read -r l_file; do a_files+=("$l_file"); done < <(grep -Pls -- '^([^#
]+)? TMOUT ' \ /etc/*bashrc /etc/profile /etc/profile.d/*.sh /etc/profile.d/sh.local \ /usr/etc/profile.d/*.sh /usr/etc/profile.d/sh.local 2>/dev/null) l_set=0 for l_file in "${a_files[@]}"; do l_val="$(grep -Po -- '^([^#
]+)? TMOUT=\d+ ' "$l_file" | awk -F= '{v=$2} END{if (NR>0) print v}')" [ -z "$l_val" ] && continue l_set=1 l_ro="$(grep -P -- '^\h*(typeset\h\- xr\hTMOUT=\d+|([^#
]+)? readonly\h+TMOUT )' "$l_file")" l_exp="$(grep -P -- '^\h*(typeset\h\- xr\hTMOUT=\d+|([^#
]+)? export ([^#
]+ )?TMOUT )' "$l_file")" [ "$l_val" -gt 0 ] && [ "$l_val" -le "$l_max" ] || echo "$l_file: TMOUT=$l_val is out of range (must be >0 and <=$l_max)" [ -n "$l_ro" ] || echo "$l_file: TMOUT is not marked readonly" [ -n "$l_exp" ] || echo "$l_file: TMOUT is not exported" done [ "$l_set" -eq 0 ] && echo "TMOUT is not set in any shell initialization file" } Nothing should be returned.

Run the following script to list any duplicate UIDs:
#!/usr/bin/env bash
{
while read -r l_count l_uid; do
if [ "$l_count" -gt 1 ]; then
echo -e "Duplicate UID: \"$l_uid\" Users: \"$(awk -F: '($3 == n) {
print $1 }' n=$l_uid /etc/passwd | xargs)\""
fi
done < <(cut -f3 -d":" /etc/passwd | sort -n | uniq -c)
}
Nothing should be returned.

